**Read:** the assigned microscopy, chemical-analysis and XPS material. Use the original teaching models as independent benchmarks before studying more complex observations.

The following three investigations examine spatial coverage, residue identity and quantity, and film depth/heterogeneity. They share the problem of inferring an unobserved state through a method with finite information.

Use benign hypothetical specimens, simulated data or appropriately documented published observations. Preserve source versions, units, sample boundaries and analysis choices. A numerical reconstruction should say exactly which published result or teaching model it reproduces.

**Work:** Carry each investigation through an uncertainty account and at least one competing explanation. Choose a discriminating observation and explain its value for the object's useful function. The three paths rejoin in a review before continuing into qualification and monitoring.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
**Continue in clean manufacturing:** [Investigate spatial contamination with an image model](Investigate-spatial-contamination-with-an-image-model.lingua#r_40E0NH081H4YB5EHK7ANPHD1CS), [Join residue collection, calibration and chemical identity](Join-residue-collection-calibration-and-chemical-identity.lingua#r_3R13K2NKR59R9NW72JF9JZ64B3), [Distinguish a uniform film from a heterogeneous surface](Distinguish-a-uniform-film-from-a-heterogeneous-surface.lingua#r_0ERWDRZST63TEJWACK3AP963H6).

